<div class="section" style="padding-bottom:56px;max-width:720px;">
  <h1>About {{ config.narrative.project_name }}</h1>
  <p class="section__intro" style="max-width:none;">
    A small pipeline that pulls public UK datasets, filters them down to one town, and turns
    the results into short, human-approved articles — automated local data journalism, running
    entirely on free infrastructure.
  </p>

  <section style="margin-top:36px;">
    <h2 style="font-size:20px;">Why it started here</h2>
    <p style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--text-muted);">
      This project is a rebuild of a 2020 Salisbury open data effort — restarted so the same
      pipeline can run for any English or Welsh town by writing a new configuration file, rather
      than forking any code. Salisbury is simply the first place it runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:32px;">
    <h2 style="font-size:20px;">How it runs</h2>
    <p style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--text-muted);">
      Scheduled jobs fetch data, deterministic code in Python and R computes the numbers, and a
      static site is published to GitHub Pages — no server to maintain. An AI assistant is used
      sparingly, only to phrase a short article when a figure moves enough to be worth writing
      about, and only after a human signs off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:32px;">
    <h2 style="font-size:20px;">Built to be reused</h2>
    <p style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--text-muted);">
      Every locality-specific detail lives in a single configuration file. It's designed so that
      bringing this to another town means writing that file, not touching the pipeline itself —
      though that hasn't been proven with an actual second-locality test yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p style="margin-top:36px;">
    <a href="https://github.com/wrightdk/SODP2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the source on GitHub &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</div>
